{
  "id": "final-stock-span",
  "questionId": "final-stock-span",
  "slug": "final-stock-span",
  "title": "Stock Span (Batch)",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Monotonic stack",
  "patterns": [
    "monotonic stack",
    "array"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "walking backwards from every single day is wasteful - once you know a price was beaten by a smaller span, can you skip past that whole stretch instead of re-checking it day by day?",
    "keep a stack of days whose span hasn't been 'absorbed' yet. what should happen to a day on the stack once a later, higher price arrives?",
    "while the stack's top price is <= today's price, pop it and add its span to today's running span, since today's span swallows that whole stretch at once",
    "today's span starts at 1 (for itself), grows by absorbing popped spans, and then today gets pushed onto the stack with its own final span"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "resetting the span counter incorrectly and not accumulating the popped spans before pushing the current day",
    "using strict < instead of <= when comparing to the stack top, which would undercount spans with ties",
    "storing just prices on the stack without also storing each day's already-computed span, forcing extra recomputation"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given the daily price of a stock as an array, in chronological\norder.\n\n**Return an array containing the span for every day, where a day's span is the number of consecutive days ending on that day, counting today and going backwards, during which the price was less than or equal to today's price.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= prices.length <= 10^4",
      "1 <= prices[i] <= 10^5",
      "the span for a day always includes that day itself, so every span is at least 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "strictly increasing prices, where every day's span extends all the way back to day 0",
      "strictly decreasing prices, where every span is 1",
      "a single day of prices"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "prices = [100, 80, 60, 70, 60, 75, 85]",
        "output": "[1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 4, 6]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 4, 6]",
        "explanation": "on day 5 (price 75), the prior days 60, 70, 60 are all <= 75 but day 1 (price 80) is not, so the span is 4"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "prices = [10, 4, 5, 90, 120, 80]",
